☐ Every earlier phase and exit gate is complete.

## Evidence ledger

Record commands, results, important observations, and relevant file paths here
as the work proceeds.

### Phase 0 evidence

- Completed on 2026-07-24 against repository root
  `D:\CODE\DFHack\DwarfSpec`, branch `main`, origin
  `https://github.com/dsisco11/DwarfSpec.git`.
- The initial `git status --short` output was empty. There were no unrelated
  worktree changes to preserve.
- Baseline syntax command: `.\tools\Check-Lua.ps1`.
  Result: `Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting checks passed for 202 files.`
- Baseline full unit command: `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
  Result: `274 successes / 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 pending`.
- `mount.host_screen` currently combines these responsibilities:
  - ownership registration: `mount_context.lua` accepts
    `adapter_result.host_screen`, stores it on the mount, and inserts it into
    `owned_screens`;
  - ownership release: `cleanup_mount()` clears the reference, while each
    current adapter dismisses its active host during scoped cleanup;
  - interaction routing: `ds.lua` returns `host_screen` for both omitted and
    subject interaction targets, then uses it for input and redraw;
  - viewport mutation: `mount_adapters.lua` calls `host_screen:onResize()`;
  - failure-tree rooting: diagnostics prefer `mount.host_screen` over
    `mount.root`;
  - rendering: all existing adapters install instance-level `onRender`
    instrumentation on the same screen before returning it as `host_screen`.
    There is no separate interaction or render-observer field.
  - construction placeholders and cleanup resets in `mount_context.lua` do not
    add another responsibility; they initialize or release the same combined
    field.
- Current table-only assumptions are:
  - `subject.lua` requires `type(view) == 'table'`, weakly retains that view,
    annotates `Subject:raw()` as returning a table, and has no adapter or
    locator;
  - `mount_context.lua` requires an adapter root table, indexes ownership by
    raw Lua object, recursively walks `view.subviews`, identifies children by
    `view_id`, and resolves retained subjects from the weak raw-view reference;
  - diagnostics directly read Lua-view `_type`, `view_id`, `visible`, `active`,
    `focus`, `frame_rect`, `frame_body`, `text`, `tooltip`, and `subviews`;
  - tree capture recursively walks only `subviews`, so it cannot enumerate a
    native `widget_container`;
  - `ds.inspect()` sends the raw view directly to Lua-view diagnostics,
    `ds.move_pointer()` requires `frame_body`, and
    `ds.capture_view_tree()` sends `mount.root` directly to diagnostics;
  - current render instrumentation separately requires a Lua table with an
    `onRender` method and uses `rawget`/`rawset`, so it cannot instrument native
    viewscreen userdata.
- Existing public and internal behavior recorded from the current source:
  - `ds.mount(component, options)` always classifies and prepares an explicit
    component, selects its category adapter, enforces one current mount, waits
    for the first tracked render, refreshes the Lua view tree, and returns a
    root subject;
  - `ds.root()` creates a new weakly retained subject for the current
    `mount.root`;
  - `ds.get(path)` accepts only a nonempty slash-delimited string, walks direct
    `subviews` by exact `view_id`, rejects malformed/reserved/duplicate IDs,
    and reports at most 12 sorted available child IDs;
  - `Subject:raw()` succeeds only while the original mount remains current and
    the weak raw table remains in the mount's `view_mounts` ownership map;
  - `ds.input(keys, subject)` requires an active `host_screen`, resolves its
    shown native screen, and calls `gui.simulateInput()`;
  - `ds.mouseInput(button, action)` uses the current mount's host screen and
    current virtual pointer, defaults button actions to `EInputState.CLICK`,
    supports explicit down/up and wheel input, temporarily installs GPS mouse
    coordinates, and retains explicit button state for cleanup;
  - `ds.move_pointer(subject, anchor)` requires a Lua `frame_body`, supports
    center and four corners, installs the virtual pointer, and waits for the
    resulting render through the common mutation path;
  - `ds.redraw(subject, options)` calls `host_screen:invalidate()` and waits for
    a later tracked render unless `options.wait == false`.
- Native API verification used the exact local DFHack
  `D:\CODE\DFHack\dfhack-53.15-r1` source plus the
  [DFHack Lua API reference](https://docs.dfhack.org/en/53.11-r1/docs/dev/Lua%20API.html#screens):
  - `Gui::getCurViewscreen(bool)` returns the topmost viewscreen and optionally
    skips dismissed screens;
  - `Gui::getDFViewscreen(bool, viewscreen)` walks past DFHack-owned screens,
    so equality between the two calls is the correct attachment precondition;
  - `df::viewscreen` contains an embedded `df::widget_container widgets`;
  - typed DF references are Lua userdata, and equality compares type and
    pointer value even when separate wrapper userdata instances are produced;
  - `dfhack.gui.getWidget()` accepts a widget container followed by string
    names or zero-based integral indices, supports chained traversal, and
    returns no value when a segment is missing or an intermediate child is not
    a container;
  - `dfhack.gui.getWidgetChildren()` returns the container's `children` in
    vector order;
  - `df::widget` exposes `parent`, `rect`, `name`, and `flag`, and inherits the
    virtual `get_rect()` method;
  - the native flags are `VISIBILITY_ACTIVE` and `VISIBILITY_VISIBLE`;
  - verified type-specific fields include text `str`, scroll `scroll` and
    `num_visible`, tab `cur_idx`, radio-row `selected_idx`, dropdown
    `cur_selected`, tooltip data, and better-button `display_string`;
  - DFHack's native render interpose calls the original native render first,
    then calls `plugins.overlay.render_viewscreen_widgets(vs_name, vs)`;
  - the Lua overlay dispatcher renders the matched viewscreen overlays and then
    the `all` overlays, making successful return from that function the proposed
    native-plus-overlay completion boundary;
  - `gui.simulateInput(screen, keys)` is DFHack's supported input path, and
    `dfhack.screen.invalidate()` requests a repaint.
- Attachment must still perform explicit runtime capability checks for:
  callable current/DF viewscreen getters; exact screen equality; a valid
  `widgets` container; callable widget helpers; callable global invalidation;
  a callable overlay render dispatcher; and successful invalidate-and-observe
  completion. Widget inspection must treat `get_rect()` and type-specific
  extractors as per-widget capabilities with documented fallbacks.
- Injectable fixture boundaries for later implementation are:
  - a native GUI service for current/DF screen acquisition, widget lookup,
    child enumeration, and normal input dispatch;
  - a native screen service for invalidation and current window dimensions;
  - a native widget adapter with explicit identity comparison, type, hierarchy,
    bounds, flags, and safe optional field access, allowing unit fixtures to use
    opaque identity tokens while production uses userdata;
  - an overlay service that exposes registry state and a replaceable render
    dispatcher without changing overlay lifecycle;
  - the existing pointer adapter behind injected virtual-pointer, GPS, enabler,
    and button-state access;
  - a native render observer supplied with the overlay dispatcher, pinned
    screen identity predicate, and the already injected render tracker;
  - mount selection kept injectable through the existing boundary,
    adapter-factory, subject-module, render-tracker, and failure-reporter seams.
- Existing focused regression files that must remain green during the shared
  refactor:
  `tests/unit/component_boundary_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/mount_context_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/mount_adapters_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/ds_mount_commands_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/subject_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/render_instrumentation_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/render_tracker_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/automation_diagnostics_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/automation_interaction_support_spec.lua`,
  `tests/unit/overlay_mount_spec.lua`, and
  `tests/unit/automation_cleanup_spec.lua`.
- Only this planning document changed while completing Phase 0; no production
  source, declaration, test, package, or runtime file was modified.

### Phase 1 evidence

- Added `src/dwarfspec/interaction_target.lua` with a documented
  `dwarfspec.InteractionTarget` contract:
  `assert_current(operation)`, `native_screen(operation)`, `invalidate()`, and
  idempotent non-owning `cleanup()`.
- Added `src/dwarfspec/lua_view_adapter.lua` with documented
  `dwarfspec.SubjectAdapter`, `dwarfspec.SubjectSource`, and
  `dwarfspec.LuaViewAdapter` contracts. The adapter owns component traversal,
  exact identity, containment, child names, class labels, bounds, evaluated
  visibility/activity, focus, text, tooltip, optional inspection fields, and
  stable inspection.
- `Subject` now retains a copied descriptor containing mount ID, source, path
  segments, adapter, captured identity, and diagnostic path. It no longer
  retains a weak raw-view field or requires the raw target to be a table.
- Every subject command still enters through `resolve_subject()`. Resolution
  now reacquires the path through the descriptor adapter, compares exact
  captured identity, verifies containment, and rejects removal or replacement
  at the same path.
- Mount cleanup releases every retained descriptor, then releases the
  interaction target and subject adapter without asking either abstraction to
  dismiss or mutate the component screen.
- All widget, overlay, and complete-screen adapters now return independent
  `host_screen`, `interaction_target`, and `subject_source` values.
  `host_screen` remains the owning lifecycle and viewport value;
  public input/redraw commands no longer read it.
- `owned_screens` receives only the adapter's owning `host_screen`.
  Tests explicitly prove that neither the interaction target nor subject source
  is entered into ownership evidence.
- Public inspection, pointer geometry, tree capture, keyboard input, mouse
  input, clicks, text input, and redraw now route through the selected subject
  adapter or interaction target.
- Diagnostics accept an optional subject adapter and capture the component
  source root instead of treating the owned host screen as the subject root.
  The legacy no-adapter path remains available for existing direct diagnostics
  callers.
- Lazy Lua-view properties now preserve a successful boolean `false` result
  instead of converting it to the prior unavailable-value sentinel. This is
  required for the adapter's evaluated visibility/activity contract and is
  covered directly.
- Added `tests/unit/interaction_target_spec.lua` and
  `tests/unit/lua_view_adapter_spec.lua`. Extended the existing subject, mount
  context, mount adapter, and public-command suites without removing behavioral
  assertions.
- Added explicit coverage for:
  interaction currentness and native resolution; invalidation; cleanup without
  dismissal; inactive-screen rejection; adapter traversal and inspection;
  exact raw identity; descriptor release; dynamic nested lookup; removal;
  replacement at the same path; root/source identity; input and redraw routing;
  pointer behavior; owned-screen counts; full component cleanup; and continued
  rejection of `ds.mount()` with no component.
- Focused command:
  `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1 --filter <contract filter>`, run sequentially for
  `owned%-screen interaction target`, `Lua view subject adapter`,
  `DwarfSpec subject commands`, `DwarfSpec mount context`,
  `DwarfSpec mount adapters`, `DwarfSpec public mount commands`, and
  `automation mount diagnostics`.
  Result: 51 successes total, zero failures, errors, or pending tests.
- Full unit command: `.\tools\Run-UnitTests.ps1`.
  Result: `281 successes / 0 failures / 0 errors / 0 pending`.
- Syntax command: `.\tools\Check-Lua.ps1`.
  Result: `Lua 5.4.6 syntax and formatting checks passed for 206 files.`
- `git diff HEAD --check` passed.
- No native attachment overload, subject-source enum, native userdata adapter,
  or native render observer is exposed in this work. The public-command suite
